\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Immigrants' Share Of Labor Hits High --- Native-born workers' slice is shrinking as retirements rise and newcomers fill the gap\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gabriel T. Rubin and Rosie Ettenheim \par \uc2 553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Foreign-born workers' share of the U.S. labor force rose last year to the highest level in 27 years of records, as labor demand surged and the pandemic faded.\par \par \uc2 People born outside the U.S. made up 18.1% of the overall labor force, up from 17.4% the prior year and the highest level in data back to 1996, the Labor Department said in its annual report on foreign-born workers. The number of immigrants in the labor force -- those working or actively looking for jobs -- rose by 1.8 million, or 6.3%, to 29.8 million in 2022.\par \par \uc2 More foreign-born people joined the labor force than native-born Americans, accounting for more than half of the 3.1 million overall gain last year, the report said. There were roughly 164 million workers age 16 or older in the U.S. labor force last year.\par \par \uc2 Sluggish U.S. population growth and accelerating baby-boomer retirements during the pandemic created labor shortages in many industries, increasing job opportunities for foreign-born workers.\par \par \uc2 Overall labor-force participation -- the share of people over 16 who are working or seeking work -- has recently increased but remains below prepandemic levels. "Any real gains we're seeing in the labor force are coming from immigrants -- they're a buoy," said Elizabeth Crofoot, senior economist at Lightcast, a labor-market data firm.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department's report was based on a monthly survey of 60,000 households and doesn't break down foreign-born workers by country of origin or legal status. The figures include legally admitted immigrants, refugees, temporary residents such as students and temporary workers, and immigrants lacking permanent legal status.\par \par \uc2 The immigrant labor force increased 5% from 2019 to 2022, while the number of native-born people in the labor force declined 0.5%, the data show.\par \par \uc2 That has put a strain on employers trying to hire across the economy, butparticularly for in-person jobs such as restaurant and construction workers, truck drivers and home-health aides -- roles that immigrants are helping fill. The jobless rate for foreign-born workers was 3.4% last year, below 3.7% unemployment for native-born Americans.\par \par \uc2 "Immigrants go where there are plenty of jobs, not where jobs are scarce," Crofoot said. "They're very much complements to native-born workers."\par \par \uc2 Foreign-born people increased their share of the workforce despite a pandemic slowdown in immigration that left the U.S. with fewer immigrants than if prepandemic trends had continued.\par \par \uc2 By the end of 2021, the U.S. population had around two million fewer people than it would have had if not for the pandemic, according to a 2022 \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysis, and the labor force had around 1.6 million fewer. Immigration has rebounded since then.\par \par \uc2 Foreign-born workers are often concentrated in lower-paying industries because of skills, education or language barriers, with notable exceptions in fields such as tech and medicine.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign-born workers often don't have the time or the wealth to enter a costly training process" for a better-paying job, said Ryan Nunn, a researcher at the Minneapolis Federal Reserve.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw409\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6135\pichgoal9600
