\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- Word on the Street: Once a Military Barrier, Now a Tool Of Protest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Zimmer \par \uc2 625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Picket]\par \par \uc2 Since May 2, when the Writers Guild of America called for a strike after contract talks fell through with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , WGA union members have joined picket lines in Los Angeles and New York City. Picketers have gathered every day in front of the offices of major studios, streaming services and networks, showing their solidarity with chants and creative signs.\par \par \uc2 Picketing has long served as a union tactic during strikes to dissuade people from entering a workplace and to bring attention to the union's demands. But before the word "picket" got associated with labor protests, it was chiefly a military term.\par \par \uc2 "Picket" comes from the French "piquet," which in turn is formed from the verb "piquer," meaning "to pierce," and the diminutive suffix "-et," so it roughly means "little piercing thing." The same root gives us "pique" -- originally for something that pricks or stings but eventually applied to arousing feelings of vexation, anger or curiosity. "Pike," a name for a long pointed spear, has the same piercing origin.\par \par \uc2 The French word "piquet" dates back to the 14th century, referring to a pointed stake. Early on, the term took on military significance for the stakes often used to construct a stockade or other defensive barrier.\par \par \uc2 The earliest known appearance in English, according to the Oxford English Dictionary, is from a 1687 treatise by the scholar John Taylor, "Thesaurarium Mathematicae, or The Treasury of Mathematicks." Taylor applied geometric principles to fortifying structures of various shapes and sizes. In a section on hexagonal fortifications, he advised how to "set piquets" around a perimeter.\par \par \uc2 By the early 18th century, the English spelling had changed to "picket," and it was being used as a noun or a verb for fixing stakes in the ground, for instance to secure a tent or tether a horse. "Picket" was also used for a kind of army punishment where the offender would be forced to stand on one foot perched on top of a stake.\par \par \uc2 Since "pickets" could be used for making a fence around an encampment, the word got extended to soldiers who served a similar defensive purpose. An "outlying picket" was a small detachment of troops sent out to scout for an enemy's approach, while an "inlying picket" stayed back in camp, ready to guard against a surprise attack.\par \par \uc2 By the early 19th century, "picket" came into civilian use for people stationed outside a factory or other workplace during a labor strike, based on the resemblance to a military-style picket. The OED cites an 1818 archival record about an English mill strike: "The men ordered to attend the mills in a morning . . . are called Pickets, & are for the very purpose of intimidating the hands going to work."\par \par \uc2 The "picket line," formerly a defensive military boundary, made its way into American labor usage by the late 19th century. An April 1893 article in the St. Louis Post-Dispatch told of a strike among union workers at the Chicago World's Fair, where delegates were stationed around the fairgrounds "in regular picket line formation." The verb "picket" made an appearance in the same article: "Each side of the street leading to the grounds was picketed on both sides of the walk to make the canvass of incoming workers as complete as possible."\par \par \uc2 While people engaged in picketing have been called "pickets," nowadays the noun is typically used to modify other nouns, as in the "picket lines and picket signs" that Marvin Gaye sang about in his 1971 soul classic, "What's Going On." Or, as one current Writers Guild slogan puts it, "Pencils down, picket signs up."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230513ej5d0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
