\par\par \b \uc2 Writers' Strike Clouds Ad Sales\b0\par\par\uc2 By Suzanne Vranica and Joe Flint \par \uc2 923 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The writers' strike is casting a shadow over the TV business's annual advertising bazaar, which was already under pressure from a softening ad market and eroding television ratings.\par \par \uc2 Due to concerns over picketing writers, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  canceled its in-person presentation to advertisers, which was scheduled to take place next week at the \uc2 Netflix\uc2 -owned Paris Theater near New York's Central Park. Instead, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  will make its presentation -- its first-ever pitch to advertisers as part of its efforts to boost its new ad-supported version -- virtually.\par \par \uc2 Other companies are sticking to their plans, but the events are expected to be more low-key and feature fewer stars. Many in the entertainment industry are wary of being perceived as not being supportive of the writers, who have been on strike since May 1. The strike began after the Writers Guild of America and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  were unable to come to terms on a new three-year deal.\par \par \uc2 Companies presenting next week include \uc2 Comcast\uc2 's \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 , \uc2 Fox\uc2 , \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  -- the parent of HBO Max, TNT, TBS and CNN -- and \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2 , whose media properties include ABC and \uc2 Disney\uc2 +.\par \par \uc2 The goal: persuade brands to commit to buying billions of dollars in commercial time for the upcoming TV season.\par \par \uc2 Ad buyers expect the writers' strike -- the first since 2007-08 -- to add even more complexity to the negotiations, which were already expected to be tough given the advertising pullback and the growing number of traditional channels, streaming platforms and tech companies vying for TV ad dollars.\par \par \uc2 Some programming was immediately affected by the strike. Late-night talk shows such as ABC's "Jimmy Kimmel Live" and NBC's "Late Night with Seth Meyers" went into reruns earlier this month, while actress Drew Barrymore dropped out of hosting the MTV Movie & TV Awards.\par \par \uc2 The strike casting doubt on the availability of fresh content for the fall television season adds to the challenges.\par \par \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  CEO \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2  said the ad market "continues to be challenging" during its first quarter earnings call with analysts earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 , which includes networks such as \uc2 CBS\uc2 , \uc2 MTV\uc2  and Nickelodeon, recently reported that its TV ad revenue fell 11% during the latest quarter, due in part to lower spending by technology and insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2  parent \uc2 Comcast\uc2  last month said the advertising market was affected by a tough macroeconomic environment.\par \par \uc2 "It's an economic storm we haven't really seen before," Mark Marshall, \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 's interim chair global advertising, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Marshall got the new, interim title on Friday morning, after \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2  announced that its head of advertising, \uc2 Linda Yaccarino\uc2 , was leaving the company. Ms. Yaccarino was picked by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to succeed him as the chief executive of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fox\uc2  ad-sales head \uc2 Marianne Gambelli\uc2  said she expects many advertisers to hold back on spending during the upfront in favor of the scatter market, which is industry slang for the week-to-week sale of advertising inventory.\par \par \uc2 Insider Intelligence expects upfront ad spending will fall by 3.4% to $18.6 billion for the 2023-2024 season. The research firm cited uncertainty in the economy, falling TV ratings and cord-cutting as part of the reason for the decline.\par \par \uc2 Paul Verna, Intelligence's principal analyst, said the firm doesn't expect the strike to have a significant impact on ad spending, provided it is resolved within the next few months.\par \par \uc2 Traditional TV network advertising has been under increasing pressure as ad dollars have been migrating to streaming services and other digital players such as \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , \uc2 Roku\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , as brands try to find audiences who have flocked to other content providers.\par \par \uc2 "There has been long term erosion of TV viewing for both broadcast and cable networks for some time," said Vincent Letang, executive vice president of global market intelligence at \uc2 Magna\uc2 , a media research and ad buying firm. Overall ad spending on national TV networks shrank by 4.8% last year to $38.4 billion and is expected to decline 6.7% this year, according to \uc2 Magna\uc2  estimates.\par \par \uc2 Some media companies have been able to mitigate the declines in ad revenue by continuing to raise ad prices and by pushing their other properties including free ad-supported streaming platforms such as Paramount's Pluto TV and \uc2 Fox\uc2 's Tubi.\par \par \uc2 Network executives have been working overtime to reassure advertisers that the strike won't have a significant impact, ad buyers said. During its earnings call last week, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  CEO \uc2 Bob Bakish\uc2  expressed confidence in the company's ability to weather the strike, citing the many "levers" it can pull and the ample amount of content it has "in the can."\par \par \uc2 Advertisers aren't convinced. Uncertainty over how much fresh content there will be in the fall are making some of them skittish about making big commitments now.\par \par \uc2 "I have told the media companies that I am worried," said Matt Sweeney, chief investment officer for the U.S. at GroupM, \uc2 WPP PLC\uc2 's ad-buying giant. He expects the strike to accelerate the migration of TV ad dollars to streaming services such as \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and the streaming properties owned by TV network owners such \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 's Paramount+ and \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 's Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  plans to reduce its spending by $300 million this year, according to people familiar with the matter, as the streaming giant continues its push to improve profitability in a competitive market.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230513ej5d0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
