\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: American's Pilots Vote to Authorize Strike --- Airline says talks with union over contract are progressing; 'the finish line is in sight'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alison Sider \par \uc2 655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pilots at \uc2 American Airlines Group\uc2  voted overwhelmingly in favor of a measure authorizing union leaders to call for a strike, though the airline said negotiations are still progressing.\par \par \uc2 The vote results announced Monday don't mean a walkout is imminent or inevitable. Federal law makes it difficult for airline unions to go on strike.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Allied Pilots Association\uc2 , which represents some 15,000 \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  pilots, said 99% of votes were in favor of authorizing a strike, with 96% of its members voting.\par \par \uc2 The airline said Monday that it believes an agreement could come together quickly, with a handful of matters remaining to resolve, and characterized the vote as an expression of pilots' sense of urgency.\par \par \uc2 "The finish line is in sight," a spokeswoman said. "We understand that a strike authorization vote is one of the important ways pilots express their desire to get a deal done and we respect the message of voting results."\par \par \uc2 Capt. Ed Sicher, the union's president, said that while pilots are willing to strike if necessary, reaching a deal for a new contract is still possible. He said pilots had proposed scheduling and work rules that could benefit pilots and make the airline more reliable.\par \par \uc2 "The best outcome is for APA and management to agree on an industry-leading contract -- achieved through good-faith bargaining," Mr. Sicher said.\par \par \uc2 American has already pledged to match pay increases outlined in a deal that \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  struck with its own pilots earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Pilots across North America have been negotiating for new labor deals, resuming talks that were largely put on hold during the Covid-19 pandemic. Such talks often become heated, but an industrywide shortage of pilots that emerged after carriers encouraged thousands to retire during the pandemic has given pilots more leverage to push for big pay increases and improvements in working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Pilots at \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  are set to begin voting on a similar strike authorization measure this month.\par \par \uc2 Such votes have been a common part of the choreography of airline negotiations, though actual walk-offs have been rare in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Pilots at Delta and \uc2 Alaska Air Group\uc2  endorsed similar measures to give union leadership a mandate to call for strikes last year before eventually coming to terms with management.\par \par \uc2 The law that governs U.S. airline labor contracts requires that both sides exhaust all efforts to resolve their disputes before workers can strike. The \uc2 National Mediation Board\uc2  would have to agree that the talks had reached an impasse, and offer the sides a chance to arbitrate before releasing them into a 30-day "cooling off" period.\par \par \uc2 American had hoped it reached a deal with its pilots last fall, but union leaders rejected a tentative proposal that would have included pay raises of 20% over two years. \uc2 United Airlines Holdings\uc2  pilots in November turned down a deal that would have boosted pay by more than 14.5% over 18 months.\par \par \uc2 The deal reached by pilots at Delta earlier this year set a new high-water mark. It included raises of at least 34% over its four-year term.\par \par \uc2 American Chief Executive \uc2 Robert Isom\uc2  quickly responded, telling American's pilots in a video message in March that the airline is prepared to match Delta's pay rates and use the same profit-sharing formula as Delta's pilots.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Isom said pay increases would total 40% on average in the fourth year of American's proposed deal. A narrow-body jet captain at the top of the scale would make $475,000 a year by then; a wide-body captain could earn up to $590,000.\par \par \uc2 He said American would also make significant improvements to the way it constructs trips and other quality-of-life measures pilots have prioritized.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230502ej520000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
