\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- America Pays a High Price for Low Wages --- For decades the U.S. has used wage subsidies to support the country's lowest-paid workers -- a welfare system that keeps the poor down, primarily benefits the wealthy and undermines technological innovation.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Lind \par \uc2 2,365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In "The Wealth of Nations," the founding text of free-market economics, Adam Smith took it for granted that workers should be paid enough to cover the living costs of themselves and their dependents. "A man must always live by his work, and his wages must at least be sufficient to maintain him," wrote Smith. "They must even upon most occasions be somewhat more, otherwise it would be impossible for him to bring up a family, and the race of such workmen could not last beyond the first generation."\par \par \uc2 In the last half-century, policy makers of both parties in the U.S. have successfully refuted Adam Smith. It turns out that it is indeed possible to pay wages to workers that are too low for their own maintenance, much less that of their families. This depends on using means-tested welfare programs like the earned-income tax credit (EITC), food stamps and housing vouchers, all of which compensate for wages that are too low for workers to live on.\par \par \uc2 Since its creation in 1975, the EITC, a federal wage subsidy for low-income workers and their children, has been expanded repeatedly under Democratic and Republican presidents alike. Many states also have their own versions of the EITC. Liberals like the EITC because it reduces absolute poverty, and conservatives like it because it attaches a work requirement to welfare.\par \par \uc2 But it is a myth that wage subsidies like the EITC "make work pay." On the contrary, they make taxpayers pay to rescue workers whose work does not pay enough. Nor are such wage subsidies an alternative to welfare. They are welfare in the form of cash rather than in-kind benefits like food stamps or housing vouchers. The term "refundable tax credit" is just a euphemism for redistributing income.\par \par \uc2 Wage subsidies also entangle eligible workers in the operations of the welfare state and its complex paperwork. It is puzzling that many critics of big government support the EITC, a costly federal welfare program that partly socializes the incomes of millions of American workers and makes their employers reliant on government spending.\par \par \uc2 We can call the current American labor market system the low-wage/high-welfare model. It is a success from the perspective of employers who get to pay lower wages. It is also a success for some consumers, since lower wages can mean lower prices. The losers include taxpayers, the working poor themselves and workers who are not poor but fear poverty. The low-wage model also saps the incentives for technological innovation, because cheap labor so often substitutes for labor-saving machinery.\par \par \uc2 Topping up low wages with redistributive welfare benefits does save millions of American workers from poverty, but it comes at a terrible cost to self-reliance. Underpaid workers who rely on means-tested welfare are often required to take any available job, including jobs that pay poverty wages. In a vicious cycle, the low-wage job creates welfare dependency, and the welfare state encourages low-wage work. Inevitably, this system creates a two-tier labor force, with an underclass of the working poor who derive part of their income from employers and part from government transfers.\par \par \uc2 The creation of so many low-wage jobs has far-reaching effects on the majority of Americans who are not poor. The fear of being trapped in a low-wage job fuels credential inflation, in the form of unnecessary occupational licensing and the pursuit of a college degree by young people who end up in jobs that do not or should not require one. Credentialism, in turn, prolongs the time that many Americans spend in expensive and often worthless higher education or needless occupational training, forcing them to postpone starting a family or to forgo one entirely.\par \par \uc2 American taxpayers also lose out. For the 2022 tax year, 31 million American workers and their children received an average of $2,043 in EITC benefits, for a cost to taxpayers of $63 billion. That is $63 billion in taxpayer money that could have been saved if employers had paid those workers a little more each month.\par \par \uc2 The beneficial effects often attributed to the EITC -- including lifting workers out of poverty, increased employment and reductions in child neglect and youth violence -- would also result from higher wages. And needless to say, a job that pays a living wage has a work requirement by definition.\par \par \uc2 While workers and taxpayers are the losers in the low-wage/high-welfare system, the biggest winners are a minority of Americans: low-wage employers and consumers of some goods and services provided by underpaid workers. Means-tested wage subsidies like the EITC and in-kind benefits like food stamps and Medicaid are not only vertical redistribution from rich to poor, they are also lateral transfers from all taxpayers to particular consumers who buy things made cheaper by government-subsidized labor.\par \par \uc2 Affluent Americans are disproportionately likely to hire low-wage workers to do tasks that working-class Americans do for themselves. Why should I be taxed to keep your housecleaner or gardener out of poverty, so that you can pay a lower wage for a service that benefits you alone?\par \par \uc2 It might be argued that low wages for fast-food workers are necessary to make fast food cheaper for other members of the working class, like grocery store clerks. But by this perverse logic, grocery store clerks should also be paid less so that fast-food workers can buy cheaper groceries. Fortunately, we do not live in a zero-sum economy where wages are the sole determinant of prices. Our dynamic capitalist system has raised living standards for generations not by lowering wages but by lowering prices with new technology and innovative business models.\par \par \uc2 In an American economy with a living wage, there would be no need to subsidize poor workers because nobody who worked full time would be poor. To be sure, there would still need to be a safety net for people who, for various reasons, cannot be employed full time or at all, and for others who are between jobs. But in a high-tech, high-wage economy, there should be no government wage subsidies for full-time workers.\par \par \uc2 One of the greatest harms of the low-wage/high-welfare system is the damage it does to technological innovation. Low wages deter U.S. productivity growth. Why invest in labor-saving machinery if cheap labor is available and subsidized by the government?\par \par \uc2 Many studies have shown that increases in labor costs -- whether caused by tight labor markets, higher minimum wages, immigration restrictions, unionization or other factors -- prompt businesses to adopt labor-saving technologies. As a 2022 study by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco\uc2  observed, "The option to automate may become particularly attractive in a tight labor market, in which competition for hiring adds pressure for firms to raise wages."\par \par \uc2 Thanks to the tight labor market and rising wages that followed the Covid pandemic, orders by U.S. companies for robots rose by 40% in 2022, according to the Association for Advancing Automation. Kiosks for ordering food in restaurants appeared first in Europe, not the U.S., because of higher wages for European fast-food workers. In the case of trucking, an aging workforce is encouraging trends in automation.\par \par \uc2 The effects of automation depend on whether new technology substitutes for labor or complements it, and also on demand in particular industries. In manufacturing, where machines often complement rather than replace workers, employment can remain the same or even grow if the market for the industry's goods or services expands.\par \par \uc2 Where technology substitutes for labor and demand is fixed, as in the case of kiosks in fast-food restaurants or self-service checkout in retail, workers may be replaced altogether, just as multitudes of farmworkers gave way to mechanical harvesters and typesetters lost out to publishing software. But automation also produces lower prices, which gives consumers more discretionary income to spend on goods and services in other, labor-intensive sectors. Jobs in these fields can absorb many of the workers displaced by technological progress.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, in labor-intensive sectors like healthcare or child care, which are resistant to automation, higher wages may result in higher prices. But instead of subsidizing low-wage workers to provide these services, the government could instead make them available regardless of ability to pay, through subsidies to consumers, public provision, or regulations and competition policies that lower prices.\par \par \uc2 There are limits, of course, to how high wages can go in a profitable business. But the idea that wages automatically reflect a worker's productivity is an academic theory with limited application. In the real world, firms have some discretion in how they allocate profits among shareholders, managers and workers, and the allocation is shaped by the relative bargaining power of these groups. Excessive wages can harm a firm, but so can excessive dividends and executive salaries.\par \par \uc2 If a living wage for all workers is the goal, there is plenty of room for debate about how to get there. One-size-fits-all policies like a higher federal minimum wage should be the last resort, given the wide variation in living costs across the country.\par \par \uc2 It would be better to improve the ability of workers themselves to bargain directly with employers for higher wages. This should not take the form of the "enterprise bargaining" that now prevails under federal law, with each company or each site of a company (like separate \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses or \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  coffee shops) having to unionize separately. Such a piecemeal approach can make unionized firms less competitive than their non-unionized rivals.\par \par \uc2 In many other countries, labor law permits sectoral or multiemployer bargaining, among representatives of all the employers and all the workers in a particular occupation or industry. Because all employers in that sector are covered by the same agreements on wages, schedules and benefits, no employers are disadvantaged. Such bargaining can free workers from needing to belong to a union at all. In France, most workers are not unionized, but the majority in many occupations are automatically covered by agreements negotiated among employers and organized labor.\par \par \uc2 Different models of labor representation are appropriate for different industries. In sectors with small firms employing low-wage workers who are hard to organize, wage boards -- convened by local or state governments with representatives of employers, labor and perhaps government and consumers -- can set minimum wages and standards. Several states have wage board laws dating back a century. In 2015, Gov. Andrew Cuomo dusted off New York's old wage board statute to push through an increase in the state's minimum wage for fast-food workers.\par \par \uc2 There is a risk that rising wages can lead to "wage-push inflation," as happened in the U.S. in the 1960s and '70s. The approach to this problem favored by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been to use higher interest rates to engineer a recession, creating mass unemployment to ensure that desperate workers will accept any wages they are offered. In addition to devastating workers, such policies have wiped out countless businesses and wrecked entire industries.\par \par \uc2 But less drastic measures could achieve similar ends without so much collateral damage. In a different labor setting, where workers enjoy more bargaining power and have more of a stake, there would be incentives for them to moderate wage demands, at least for a time, in negotiations with employers and government. Artificially induced recessions aren't the only possible solution.\par \par \uc2 Any reforms to increase the bargaining power of workers, collectively or individually, are pointless if employers can easily neutralize their effects by means of global labor arbitrage-off-shoring tasks to low-wage foreign workers or importing immigrant workers. Most low-wage American workers are employed in jobs like retail, food service, construction and low-end healthcare. They can't be undercut by off-shoring, but they can be affected by immigration, which reduces the pressure on employers to raise wages.\par \par \uc2 Of the millions of immigrants, authorized and unauthorized, who have entered the U.S. over the past decade, the overwhelming majority work in these sorts of low-wage jobs. Employers often claim that such immigrants do jobs that Americans won't do -- but Americans might very well do them if employers raised wages.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. can benefit from highly skilled immigrants from all over the world, but relatively few of the new immigrants have unique skills that native and foreign-born American workers lack. They appeal to employers not for their skills but for their willingness to work for low wages. Barbara Jordan, chair of President Bill Clinton's immigration commission, summarized this situation perfectly in 1996: "The commission finds no national interest in continuing to import lesser-skilled and unskilled workers to compete in the most vulnerable parts of our labor force."\par \par \uc2 Whatever the policy environment, the overall performance of the economy also has a profound influence on the labor market, and recent trends have sometimes enhanced the ability of workers to obtain higher wages. The most disadvantaged workers tended to benefit the most in the tight labor markets caused by the tech boom of the 1990s, the pre-pandemic economic recovery of the late 2010s, and more recently by the end of lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 But the ups and downs of the economy shouldn't determine whether whole classes of Americans receive decent wages. The low-wage/high-welfare system that Democrats and Republicans have supported since the late 20th century is not sustainable in the long run. The alternative is to try to achieve the long-standing aspiration of American workers and reformers: a living wage for all.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 This essay is adapted from Mr. Lind's new book, "Hell to Pay: How the Suppression of Wages Is Destroying America," which will be published on May 2 by Portfolio. He is a contributing editor of Tablet and a fellow at New America.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230429ej4t0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
