\par\par \b \uc2 5G Technology (A Special Report) --- A Satellite Phone That Works Anywhere: The U.S.-China Rivalry Makes That Harder: Both countries want to dominate the technology, and that makes having one system that works in both countries tricky\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yang Jie \par \uc2 873 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Building an affordable service that works everywhere on the planet has been the dream of mobile telephony since its early days. Companies in the U.S. and China are among those getting closer to delivering that service through satellite technology.\par \par \uc2 But there is a problem. Both of the superpowers are determined to dominate the technology, and each can wield regulations to prevent the other's satellite services from being used within its own borders.\par \par \uc2 It may be a long time before \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  can achieve his promise last year of "eliminating dead zones worldwide." Mr. Musk's rocket and satellite company, \uc2 Space Exploration Technologies Corp\uc2 ., or \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , is expanding its services for customers around the globe -- but not in China.\par \par \uc2 Lynk Global Inc., a Falls Church, Va. startup, last year obtained what it described as the first \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2  license for a service that links commercial satellites directly to standard mobile phones, without special hardware or software.\par \par \uc2 Founder Charles Miller says Lynk has commercial contracts in over 40 countries and is testing the service in others. What about China? "We haven't applied to test in China," says Mr. Miller. "We assume they would say no."\par \par \uc2 The new services work through networks of satellites orbiting the Earth. Phones can link up to one of these satellites even from the middle of a desert or ocean where there are no cell towers or base stations nearby.\par \par \uc2 While specialized satphones have been around for decades, recent services offered by the likes of \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . bring the satellite technology to regular smartphones. IPhone users with \uc2 Apple\uc2 's latest operating system can beam short distress messages from places off the cellular grid.\par \par \uc2 But each country has the authority to decide whether to allow satellite signals to transmit within its territory. "Getting such permission could become a major challenge," says Brady Wang, an analyst at Counterpoint Research.\par \par \uc2 Cellphone users can generally get roaming service when they travel abroad. That includes Americans traveling to China. Such negotiations are just beginning over satellite service. That is why \uc2 Apple\uc2 's service was initially available only in the U.S. and Canada, and more recently has expanded to parts of Europe.\par \par \uc2 Several days before \uc2 Apple\uc2  announced its service, \uc2 Huawei Technologies Co\uc2 ., the Chinese telecommunications-equipment maker that has been sanctioned by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , introduced a similar service designed for stranded users in an emergency. It works only within China.\par \par \uc2 Chinese services aren't likely to be permitted in the U.S. or closely allied countries, says Larry Press, professor of information systems at California State University, Dominguez Hills.\par \par \uc2 The Starlink service from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , which requires a special antenna, is available in parts of the U.S. as well as most of Europe, Japan, Australia and elsewhere. A map online shows plans to cover most of the globe eventually, with the exception of China and Russia. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  has teamed up with \uc2 T-Mobile US Inc\uc2 . so that \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  users with regular smartphones could tap into the \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  satellite network for basic service when they are off the cellphone grid. That service will be available in most parts of the U.S.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and Chinese companies are also moving on separate tracks in building the satellite systems behind the new services, raising the prospect of a world bifurcated between a pro-China zone using Chinese-made devices and satellites and a pro-U. S. zone using devices built from components of largely American, European, Japanese and Korean origins.\par \par \uc2 Industry analysts say the U.S. holds a significant advantage in the competition. Major U.S. tech firms and startups are participating in the race. A \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  executive recently said it has launched more than 4,000 satellites.\par \par \uc2 Most of the satellites that talk to mobile devices on the ground are deployed in low Earth orbit, or LEO, which under one definition refers to satellites less than 1,200 miles from the Earth. In general, those satellites cost less to launch and offer better transmission.\par \par \uc2 Huawei\uc2 's service uses China's self-developed Beidou satellite network, in which the satellites are more than 10,000 miles from Earth. \uc2 Huawei\uc2  users can't receive texts.Beijing has its own plans for LEO broadband with state funding, which it wants to roll out in China and in developing nations that Beijing would like to make part of its sphere of influence, according to a report published by the Washington, D.C.-based Center for Strategic and International Studies in December.\par \par \uc2 A Chinese official said in March that a state-owned aerospace company planned to build a constellation of satellites only 90 to 180 miles from Earth. The first launch is set for September.\par \par \uc2 "China is steadily progressing toward its goal of becoming a world-class space leader," said the 2023 annual threat assessment of the U.S. intelligence community, released in March.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Yang is a Wall Street Journal tech reporter based in Japan. Email her at jie.yang@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230427ej4r0002y\par }\page 