\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 The Labor Market Bends but Doesn't Break\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 545 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 The U.S. job market isn't at a rolling boil anymore, but it is hardly tepid.\par \par \uc2 That is good news for the economy, but less good for any investors hoping the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  won't raise rates at its meeting next month, much less embark on an easing campaign anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department on Thursday reported that initial claims for unemployment rose in the week ended Saturday to a seasonally adjusted 245,000 from the previous week's 240,000. That was well above the 50-plus-year low of 182,000 plumbed last September. But it was within the range that weekly claims were in the years before the pandemic, when hiring was robust and the unemployment rate was declining.\par \par \uc2 So far, at least, the raft of headline-grabbing announcements from the big tech companies, and fallout from the failures of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Signature Bank\uc2 , don't appear to have inflicted too much damage.\par \par \uc2 The latest claims figure comes from the so-called reference week for the employer and household surveys that the Labor Department uses to construct its monthly employment figures, so it suggests that the April jobs report won't show much deterioration in job growth. Indeed, initial claims during March's reference week at 247,000 were a bit higher.\par \par \uc2 The claims figures are also consistent with other evidence that the job market is moderating, but still quite strong. Indexes of overall U.S. job openings and new job openings from job-listing site Indeed are down from last year, but still significantly higher than they were before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's beige book from the Fed -- the collection of anecdotal reports on the economy gathered by regional Fed banks that comes out eight times a year -- said that employment growth "moderated somewhat," noting that the small number of mass layoff announcements "were centered at a subset of the largest companies."\par \par \uc2 So it looks like the labor market is on a gradual glide path that, if it continues, will take job growth down to the point that employers are no longer experiencing severe hiring strains. That in turn should cool wage growth and put the economy in a more sustainable place.\par \par \uc2 If that sounds a bit too good to be true, maybe that is because history suggests it normally has been. In the run-up to past recessions, the weekly claims figures have often moved a bit higher -- and then suddenly jumped. The rate increases the Fed has put in place since last spring are still working their way into the economy, and the tightening in lending conditions that has come about since last month's bank failures will take time to really register, too.\par \par \uc2 Fed policy makers know all this, but with inflation still well above the 2% they are aiming for, and with the job market still tight, they will probably raise their target range on rates by another quarter of a percentage point when they meet early next month. Maybe by their following meeting, in mid-June, they will have the evidence they need to go on hold. But that is by no means a sure thing.\par \par \uc2 page,5043 \par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230421ej4l0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
