\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Federal Workers Urged Back To Offices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Linskey and Paul Kiernan \par \uc2 423 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Biden administration urged federal workers to return to their offices in new guidance that gives agencies broad discretion for how to revamp their work environments and leaves room for some continued telework.\par \par \uc2 The policy is aimed at "substantially increasing in-person work," said \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 , the Deputy Director for Management at the Office of Management and Budget in an accompanying blog post. The 19-page memo Thursday set no concrete targets for how many federal workers must come back -- or by when -- and it indicated officials would evaluate where remote work has been effective.\par \par \uc2 The administration appears to be creating a framework that ultimately could make some amount of telework a more lasting part of the workplace after the Covid-19 pandemic emergencies end. There are 2.2 million people in the federal civilian workforce.\par \par \uc2 Under the new guidelines, each agency will be required to update plans for their workforce and identify ways to measure and improve "organizational health and performance," according to Mr. Miller. Emphasis will be placed on bringing back more workers at agency headquarters, he said.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders had been nervous ahead of the announcement, but labor embraced the potential for ongoing telework of some type for many positions. "OMB's recognition that telework's merits must be appreciated apart from concerns about Covid is appropriate," said \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , which represents federal workers.\par \par \uc2 The White House isending the national emergency declarations for Covid-19next month and is facing political pressure from Republicans and even some Democratic allies to bring federal workers back to the office.\par \par \uc2 Remote work in the federal government surged during the pandemic. In an annual survey of federal employees by the \uc2 U.S. Office of Personnel Management\uc2 , 56% of respondents reported working remotely at least one day a week last year, up from 23% in 2019. The survey is voluntary and exclude ssome federal workers.\par \par \uc2 Union officials representing federal workers want telework-eligible employees to be able to negotiate for flexible working arrangements via the collective bargaining process. "Every human being hates commuting," said Jacqueline Simon, the public policy director at the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Widespread remote work has also fueled worries about the government becoming less accountable to the people it serves. Some lawmakers have complained that agencies now take longer than before to answer emails -- and never pick up the phone -- when their staffers or constituents call.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230414ej4e0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
