\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  Puts Strikers Over Students\b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats are devoted champions of state universities, only not so much the students. Consider the faculty strike at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  in New Jersey, where the political fix is in for professors.\par \par \uc2 Rutgers faculty walked off the job Monday after three employee unions launched a strike. The move has left classrooms empty across the school's three main campuses, with about 9,000 professors, adjuncts and grad-student workers represented. The university hasn't formally canceled classes, which means students face hourly uncertainty. Some professors say they'll teach virtually for the semester, some are picketing while students try to study, and others are day-to-day.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that state law gives the school a lever to end the strike. New Jersey courts have often issued injunctions to send state employees back to work while negotiations continue, and Rutgers president Jonathan Holloway mentioned the possibility of suing before professors walked out. In a guidance note on its website, the school's administration said it "may go to court to maintain university operations and protect our students."\par \par \uc2 That's when Democratic Gov. \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  intervened. An ally of public unions, he barred Rutgers on Monday from seeking an injunction to send professors back to class. A university spokeswoman told Politico that Mr. Murphy "asked us to delay taking legal action asking the courts to order strikers back to work." It's a request they couldn't refuse, as the Governor controls 20% of Rutgers' funding and appoints a majority of its board.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Murphy is now hosting contract negotiations in Trenton, where Rutgers faculty are seeking better pay and health insurance, and equal pay for adjuncts. "I'm not happy that it's come to this. I am happy that we're in the room together," the Governor said Monday, portraying himself as an honest broker.\par \par \uc2 But his intervention has already tipped the scales toward the unions. The university and faculty failed to agree after 10 months of talks, but administrators are now under political pressure to cave. The courts could have removed the strike as a bargaining chip if Mr. Murphy had let Rutgers seek an injunction.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the strike, taxpayers and tuition payers can expect higher costs, courtesy of a Governor who's supposed to be on management's side of the table. The episode harks back to former Gov. Jon Corzine, who pledged to "fight for a fair contract" for teachers in 2006. As usual, it's a "fight" against the public on behalf of unionized state employees.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230412ej4c0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
