\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- Word on the Street: From Lowering Sails to Downing Tools\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Zimmer \par \uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Strike]\par \par \uc2 All around the world, labor strikes have been serving as a potent form of popular protest. In Israel on Monday, a nationwide strike called by the country's largest labor union contributed to Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 's decision to suspend plans for a controversial judicial overhaul. In Germany, a massive transport strike brought most travel to a halt as workers sought wage increases to offset inflation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Los Angeles, a three-day strike closed public schools before an agreement was finally reached between the school district and the union representing 30,000 support staff. The 35,000 members of the city's teachers union joined the walkout in solidarity with their fellow staffers.\par \par \uc2 Labor strikes date to ancient times. Artisans building the burial chambers for Egypt's King Ramses III walked off the job in the 12th century B.C., evidently because of insufficient rations. But calling such a work stoppage a "strike" has a more modern pedigree.\par \par \uc2 The word "strike" comes from an old Germanic root meaning "to press, rub, or beat" and is etymologically related to such similarly sounding words as "stroke" and "streak." As a verb and noun, "strike" branched off in many directions semantically. By the 14th century, it developed more violent senses, like "to deal a blow" and "to make a military assault on."\par \par \uc2 Another set of meanings for "strike" had to do with putting something down or lowering it. For sailors on the high seas,"striking a sail" means hauling it down, for instance to prepare for dropping anchor. "Striking a flag," also known as "striking the colors," refers to lowering a ship's flag as a sign of salute or surrender.\par \par \uc2 In 1768, that nautical usage took on new connotations related to labor unrest in England. In April, according to The Annual Register, a journal recapping the year's events, "a body of sailors, to the number of 400 or 500, assembled at North Shields," a coastal town in northeast England. From there they proceeded to nearby Sunderland and "read a paper setting forth their grievances." Afterward, the account continued, "they went on board the several ships in that harbour, and struck (lowered down) their yards, in order to prevent them from proceeding to sea."\par \par \uc2 Later that spring, a similar protest action of striking sails brought commerce on the Thames to a halt. Demands for higher wages spread to other industries, including workers who manufactured hats. An entry in The Annual Register from May 1768 records, "This day the hatters struck, and refused to work till their wages are raised." Presumably that meant the hatters set down their tools in the same way that sailors lowered their masts to refuse to go to sea.\par \par \uc2 The "strike" as an action by a labor union or other group of workers took hold in the U.S. by the early 19th century. In 1809, as documented in a New York court case, a society of shoemakers "ordered a general strike against the masters." That eventually led to such variations as "wildcat strikes," for strikes undertaken without official authorization from union leaders, and "sit-down strikes," wherein people occupy a workplace and refuse to leave until their demands are met.\par \par \uc2 The word also got applied to stopping other kinds of activities as a form of protest. Someone on a "hunger strike" refuses to eat, while tenants on a "rent strike" refuse to pay their rent. "Strike" has even entered other European languages adopting its English meaning. In German, for instance, the word was borrowed as "streik." And when the transport strike shut down travel in Germany recently, local news outlets referred to it as a "Mega-Streik" -- that is, a "mega-strike." While "strike" may indicate a stoppage, the word itself shows no signs of halting its progress.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230401ej410000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
