\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Option to Work From Home Disappears for Millions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gwynn Guilford \par \uc2 716 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Working remotely is becoming increasingly rare a few years after the pandemic caused millions of Americans to decamp from worksites to their basements and bedrooms.\par \par \uc2 Some 72.5% of business establishments said their employees teleworked rarely or not at all last year, according to a Labor Department report released this past week. That figure climbed from 60.1% in 2021. The survey showed about 21 million more workers on-site full time in 2022, compared with the prior year. An establishment is defined as each business location -- such as an individual restaurant in a chain.\par \par \uc2 The new number is also close to the share of establishments -- 76.7% -- that said they had no employees teleworking before the Covid-19 pandemic, and that were open in February 2020, the Labor Department said. Employers recently have begun pushing harder to get staff to work on-site more often, as recession fears prompt an increased emphasis on worker productivity.\par \par \uc2 "There's a sense that innovation, creativity and collaboration can suffer when teams are apart," said Mike Steinitz, senior executive director at Robert Half. A survey by the global recruitment firm found that 92% of managers prefer their teams to work on-site.\par \par \uc2 "They believe employees are simply more productive in the office," he said. "They also feel that it's important for mentoring and training both new and existing employees."\par \par \uc2 Several large companies have said they expect their employees to report in person, more often. \uc2 Walt Disney Co\uc2 . now pushes for four days a week on-site. \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . has asked office staff to come in more.\par \par \uc2 The share of business establishments with hybrid arrangements decreased in all measured industries in 2022 from 2021, declining 13.4 percentage points across the private sector, according to the Labor Department. A particularly stark drop played out in the finance sector, including banks and brokerages. The share of financial establishments operating hybrid dropped by half, to 22% in 2022 from 44.9% in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Remote work was particularly rare for service-providing jobs, such as those in retail, restaurants and accommodations. Employment gains in those categories contributed to more employees working on jobsites. Hiring in leisure and hospitality and retail accounted for nearly 30% of the 7.7 million gain in private-sector employment since August 2021.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department survey asked about telework, which it defines as an arrangement that allows an employee to work at home, or from another remote location, by using the internet and other digital communications. The survey was conducted in August and September of 2022, while the 2021 survey was administered from July to September of that year.\par \par \uc2 Remote work remained fairly common last year in some jobs that traditionally were done in an office. In the information sector, which includes tech and media firms, 67.4% of establishments said their staff worked remotely some or all of the time. In the professional and business sector, which includes law and accounting firms, the share was 49%.\par \par \uc2 Research by economists using a different survey -- one that measures the share of days worked remotely -- suggests that remote work persisted into 2023, though it has shown signs of slipping recently.\par \par \uc2 In February 2023, 27.7% of total days worked were from home, after holding fairly steady at an average of 30% each month in 2022, according to research by economists Jose Maria Barrero of \uc2 Instituto Tecnologico Autonomo de Mexico\uc2 , Stanford's \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 , and Steven J. Davis of the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 . The share is down sharply from May 2020, when around 60% of days were remote but still more than five times the rate that prevailed before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Companies are pushing managers and professionals to return to the office for more days each week, he said. And the number of fully remote workers is shrinking. Mr. Bloom and his colleagues' survey showed that the percentage of employees working fully on-site reached 60.8% in February, up from 54.6% in November 2021.\par \par \uc2 Remote work, however, isn't likely to entirely disappear. Some 13% of current job postings are for remote positions, according to staffing firm \uc2 ManpowerGroup\uc2 . That is down from 17% in March 2022 but well above the prepandemic level of 4%.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich443\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6645
