\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Business News: \uc2 Rivian\uc2  to Relocate Engineers to Speed Up Output\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean McLain \par \uc2 761 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Electric-vehicle maker \uc2 Rivian Automotive Inc\uc2 . is relocating parts of its manufacturing engineering team to its Illinois factory as part of a reorganization aimed at speeding up production, according to a person familiar with the plan.\par \par \uc2 The reorganization is expected to be announced soon and will result in a significant portion of the engineering team that works on manufacturing-related tasks being asked to relocate from around the country to central Illinois or the company's headquarters in Irvine, Calif., the person said.\par \par \uc2 The company is in part trying to centralize more of its workforce. During the Covid-19 pandemic, \uc2 Rivian\uc2  hired engineers wherever it could find them and allowed them to work remotely, the person familiar with the plans said.\par \par \uc2 As a result its employees are scattered across the U.S. in states like Michigan, Texas and Virginia, in addition to those located at the headquarters and in Illinois, the person added.\par \par \uc2 The relocations come as \uc2 Rivian\uc2  is overhauling its sole production facility to expand both the speed and capacity of its assembly lines. The team being relocated to Normal, Ill., is responsible for the design and functioning of the factory, from the machines that help build the vehicles to layout of the assembly line.\par \par \uc2 It couldn't be learned how many employees would be affected by the reorganization.\par \par \uc2 So far, management has had some conversations with the manufacturing-engineering team, and a few dozen employees have indicated an unwillingness to move, the person said. For those who won't relocate, \uc2 Rivian\uc2  plans to offer them severance and fill vacancies created with new hires, the person said.\par \par \uc2 The company, which employs about 14,000 workers, went public in late 2021 and grew rapidly during a period where remote work was the norm. It has run into a litany of problems on the manufacturing floor, including parts shortages and other logistical snags, following the launch of its first all-electric models -- the R1T pickup truck and R1S sport-utility vehicle -- in 2021.\par \par \uc2 The consolidation of \uc2 Rivian\uc2 's manufacturing team comes as the company rushes to trim spending and refocus resources on the manufacturing side of the business.\par \par \uc2 The EV maker conducted two rounds of white-collar layoffs and pushed back the launch of a new generation of vehicles to 2026 in an effort to conserve cash and focus on its current lineup of products.\par \par \uc2 Rivian\uc2  burned through $6.6 billion in cash last year, leaving it with $11.6 billion at the end of December. The company subsequently raised an additional $1.5 billion from investors in the form of convertible bonds, and \uc2 Rivian\uc2  has said it can fund its operations through 2025. Analysts expect those cash reserves to fall by a further $6 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive Officer RJ Scaringe has said \uc2 Rivian\uc2 's recent spending cuts have been partly in response to economic concerns and tightening capital markets, and that the company needs to sharpen focus on the parts of the business best positioned to deliver near-term returns. That includes speeding up manufacturing of its current lineup of vehicles, which include a delivery van for \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Across the auto industry, companies both new and established are struggling to boost factory output of electric vehicles, as they contend with parts shortages, logistical hurdles and quality problems. \uc2 General Motors Co\uc2 . has stumbled with the launches of two key models, the GMC Hummer EV and Cadillac Lyriq, in part because of troubles getting enough battery cells.\par \par \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 . halted production of its electric F-150 Lightning pickup truck for five weeks in February and March after one of them caught fire during a predelivery quality check.\par \par \uc2 Such production challenges put car companies at risk of losing out on sales. Many EV models, including \uc2 Rivian\uc2 's, have long waiting lists with buyer interest in battery-powered vehicles booming. Currently, \uc2 Tesla Inc\uc2 . is the only car maker in the U.S. that sells EVs in high volumes.\par \par \uc2 Lucid Group Inc\uc2 . slashed its production target twice last year in response to parts supply issues, and has said some early reservation holders canceled after delays.\par \par \uc2 Rivian\uc2  has faced its share of challenges with learning the intricacies of mass manufacturing automobiles. Logjams along the assembly line and shortages of key components made it so the startup struggled for much of last year to operate its plant for a full working week. It also launched three different models at the same time, a challenge that established auto makers rarely attempt.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230325ej3p0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
