\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Los Angeles Schools And Union Reach Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 A WSJ News Roundup \par \uc2 383 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES -- The \uc2 Los Angeles Unified School District\uc2  and union leaders said Friday that they reached a deal on pay raises for bus drivers, custodians and other support staff after a three-day strike that shut down the nation's second-largest school system, the Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 The deal includes a series of retroactive raises going back to 2021 that will collectively increase worker pay by about 30%, said Max Arias, executive director of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  Local 99, the AP said.\par \par \uc2 Students had returned to classrooms Friday after the labor strike ended but before an agreement was reached for support staff.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles Mayor Karen Bass stepped in this week to help broker a deal between the district and the \uc2 SEIU\uc2 , both sides said.\par \par \uc2 The union, which represents 30,000 bus drivers, cafeteria workers, janitors, teachers' assistants and other support staff, hasn't had a contract since 2020.\par \par \uc2 United Teachers Los Angeles\uc2 , which represents 35,000 teachers, nurses, counselors and others, joined the strike in solidarity and is separately bargaining for a new contract. The unions last week said only new contracts with higher wages will guarantee the district will avoid further labor disruptions.\par \par \uc2 LAUSD\uc2  posted a message on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Thursday thanking families for their patience as the district prepared to resume classes.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of school workers and teachers wearing the purple and red union colors gathered Thursday at a final rally in a park near downtown Los Angeles. Iris Oroxon, a special-education assistant, said that before the strike, many of her colleagues at the arts and sciences magnet school in Northeast Los Angeles where she has worked for nine years didn't know her first name.\par \par \uc2 "We were invisible," Ms. Oroxon said. In her role helping care for and teach special-education students, she makes $25 an hour for 30-hour weeks.\par \par \uc2 The union had said it was seeking 30% raises during the span of a new multiyear contract, plus an additional $2 an hour for its lowest-paid workers.\par \par \uc2 Before the strike began, the district offered 23% cumulative raises retroactive to 2021, cash bonuses of 3%, healthcare for more workers and the promise of additional hours for some part-time employees.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230325ej3p00031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
