\par\par \b \uc2 Travel (A Special Report) --- The Do's and Don'ts of Combining Work and Play When Traveling: With hybrid work, it's easier than ever to blur business and leisure on the road. But it helps to follow a few simple rules.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexandra Samuel \par \uc2 1,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par D7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I never take a business trip without squeezing in some kind of personal respite or adventure. And I've yet to take a vacation without putting some hours into work.\par \par \uc2 So I've learned to make the most of this messy, delightful overlap -- by developing my own personal do's and don'ts for combining personal and professional travel. My main advice: Don't be afraid to mix work and personal travel. Thanks to the rise of hybrid work, it's a lot easier to work from the road. Simply extending a business trip by working from another city or town can give you a welcome change of pace and scenery. And if tacking on some remote work allows you to extend your time in your vacation destination, that can be worth a little boundary-blurring, too.\par \par \uc2 As for my other do's and don'ts:\par \par \uc2 DO: be clear on whether it's a vacation or a business trip. The first combination vacation/business trip my husband and I took was a mess, because we weren't clear on our priorities. Were we visiting San Francisco to connect with clients (and bringing our toddler along), or were we there for a vacation (and making time for a few meetings)? That lack of clarity meant we felt constantly pulled between work and family priorities, and we had a hard time setting boundaries on our availability for clients.\par \par \uc2 DON'T: pressure yourself to stay offline for a multiweek vacation. A recent five-day vacation hit the spot precisely because it's utterly unrealistic for me to unplug for two or three weeks. Pressuring yourself to disconnect completely from work -- if you're in a role where things can go seriously awry in your absence -- isn't a recipe for relaxation, but a recipe for stress! Better to plan around the inevitability of (some) work while on vacation, and then figure out how to keep that work from overtaking your fun.\par \par \uc2 DO: bring your children on business trips. I have brought each of my kids on business trips, from the time my youngest was just a few months old. While we've done plenty of vacations as a family, the delight of including family in business trips is getting some one-on-one time with just one child or the other. (And giving my husband some one-on-one time with whichever child stays home.) When I gave a speech at a conference held at Disney World, it was a no-brainer to bring my then-9-year-old and add a few days to the trip so that we could visit the various theme parks. And now that my children are teenagers, it's really easy to include them in any trip where they can stay solo in the hotel room while I do my work thing -- and then we can explore a new city together.\par \par \uc2 DON'T: leave your personal time unbooked. Spontaneity may be a joy when you're on an extended vacation, but if your business trip leaves only a few hours or a day free for fun, make sure to plan your fun in advance. When a recent business trip took me to Vermont for the first time in 20 years, I decided to leave my free night unbooked, so that I could go where the spirit took me -- and unfortunately, where it took me was a long, rainy drive through the dark, all the way to Montreal, because I couldn't find an unbooked hotel or a place to have a late dinner.\par \par \uc2 DO: set boundaries around your work time. I find it easier to fit work into vacation time if I set up some guardrails. I have a separate only-on-vacation email address that I use for travel logistics (so I don't have to log into my work email to retrieve a hotel booking), and I set up mail rules on my primary email account that forward high-priority messages to my vacation address. Then I decide on one or two windows a day when I'll check my "vacation" email address for those high-priority messages, or take care of other work-related tasks.\par \par \uc2 DON'T: leave your notifications on. Turn off notifications for \uc2 Slack\uc2 , Teams and other applications you use for work communication, and log out of your office email account on your phone. Ideally, turn off the new email notifications on your phone, too, so that even your vacation inbox won't ping you and trigger your work brain to turn back on.\par \par \uc2 DO: selectively add social dates to your business trips. It's delightful when business travel affords the opportunity to reconnect with friends -- as long as it doesn't exhaust you or interfere with work. After missing the chance to see my college roommate on a trip to New York (I forgot she'd moved there!), I now make a point of searching \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to remind myself of who lives in each city I'm visiting, so I can reach out to friends in advance of a trip. I've come to accept that it's better to make fewer dates, and actually connect with old friends, than to try to cram in everybody who lives in that town.\par \par \uc2 DON'T: broadcast your travels on social media. Whether I'm on the road for work or pleasure, I'm very careful about posting anything about my trip, especially beforehand: It's a recipe for awkward exchanges with friends or colleagues who want to see you while you're in town, even if you don't have time to get together. Particularly when my children were younger, I had to keep my business trips very short, and I hated to hurt the feelings of family or friends by letting them see I was in town but not making time to see them. If you need to post about your trip for professional reasons, try to save the posting until after the trip . . . when you can beg for forgiveness, rather than dodging an invitation.\par \par 