\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Los Angeles School Workers Walk Out\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sara Randazzo \par \uc2 650 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES -- Bus drivers, special-education aides, custodians and other support staff in Los Angeles schools walked off the job Tuesday, triggering a citywide strike that shut down schools for 420,000 students as workers demand higher wages.\par \par \uc2 The strike is being led by a union of 30,000 workers integral to running the nation's second-largest school district, with 35,000 teachers joining the walkout in solidarity. The closure comes after three years of pandemic-related challenges, and as both unions are in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 "We are on strike because we've had enough," Conrado Guerrero, president of Local 99 of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , said Tuesday morning outside Robert F. Kennedy Community Schools. "Enough of the disrespect. We refuse to be invisible."\par \par \uc2 Workers and union leaders said the strike is about gaining respect for an often overlooked workforce: staff who keep trash cans from overflowing, drive students to school, and help educate and keep safe some of the highest-needs students.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all the support staff in the \uc2 Los Angeles Unified School District\uc2  earn below $45,000 a year. Full-time food-service workers earn an average of $25,000, special-education workers earn $36,700, and building and grounds staff earn an average of $39,240, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Rep. Adam Schiff joined union leaders Tuesday to urge the district to pay a livable wage to workers, pointing to the high cost of living in the city. The union is seeking 30% raises across the board, plus $2 an hour more for the lowest-paid workers. The latest district offer includes a 23% salary increase, 3% bonus and improved healthcare benefits.\par \par \uc2 "We understand the frustration, a frustration that has been brewing not just for a couple of years but probably for decades," LAUSD Superintendent \uc2 Alberto Carvalho\uc2  said Monday night while announcing that the district will be closed for the duration of the strike, which is scheduled to last through Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The union and district officials have both said they are open to talking.\par \par \uc2 Technically, this week's strike isn't about the impasse in contract talks. Instead, it is a so-called unfair labor practices strike, called over alleged labor law violations the staff union says the district has committed. The union filed over a dozen grievances, accusing the district of violations ranging from managers locking cafeteria doors to prevent workers from voting on a strike to raising the price of vending-machine snacks in staff lounges.\par \par \uc2 Cecily Myart-Cruz\uc2 , the president of \uc2 United Teachers Los Angeles\uc2 , said her union couldn't stand by idly while the support staff demanded better pay.\par \par \uc2 Some parents expressed frustration over the closure of schools. "The children are the ones that are hurt by all of it," said Danna Rosenthal. She said she bristled at union leaders chanting about "shutting it down" at a rally. "It's not like we're talking about a car factory, this is an education you're shutting down."\par \par \uc2 School board members heard Tuesday from 10 mothers from Our Voice, a group of Latino parents, at the board's meeting. Evelyn Aleman, the group's leader, said many families in the district are immigrants, some undocumented, who have to stay home with their children and can't work when schools are shut down. Special-education assistant Denia Serrano said the support staff need higher pay and more respect. She said she makes $25.75 an hour during a nine-month contract, which doesn't give her enough to build savings.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown is the first labor test for Mr. Carvalho, who joined the Los Angeles district just over a year ago from Miami. "We will get through this," Mr. Carvalho said Monday night. "And at some point we will agree to a contract. But not without a necessary dialogue."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230322ej3m0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
