\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Zuckerberg Envisions Leaner, Nimble Meta\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cancel unnecessary projects. Eliminate middle management. Flatten the corporate hierarchy.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  has laid out to staff an updated vision of how he wants \uc2 Facebook\uc2  owner \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2 . to operate in what he has coined a "year of efficiency."\par \par \uc2 In a roughly 2,200-word note to employees Tuesday, punctuated by headings such as "flatter is faster" and "leaner is better," Mr. Zuckerberg, the chief executive of \uc2 Meta\uc2 , outlined several efforts to build what he described as a more focused, productive company able to withstand the current environment of economic uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 "Last year was a humbling wake-up call," Mr. Zuckerberg wrote. "The world economy changed, competitive pressures grew, and our growth slowed considerably."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg said Tuesday that the social-media company would cut 10,000 jobs over multiple rounds of layoffs in the coming months and keep an additional 5,000 open positions unfilled.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  intends to remove multiple layers of management and convert many managers into employees with no supervisory responsibilities. Those employees will then report to almost every layer of the organization, Mr. Zuckerberg said, to improve communication across the company. Low-priority projects will be killed. Internal tools will be strengthened to help software engineers write code faster.\par \par \uc2 "A leaner org will execute its highest priorities faster. People will be more productive, and their work will be more fun and fulfilling," Mr. Zuckerberg said in his email to staff.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 's latest layoffs follow an announcement in November that the company would cut more than 11,000 workers. Mr. Zuckerberg said at the time that the company had overexpanded on the misguided assumption that an increase in online activity during the pandemic would continue. "I got this wrong and I take responsibility for that," he wrote in a message to staff at the time.\par \par \uc2 "When companies face competitive threats, we know they're more likely to adopt stronger management practices and look for opportunities to create more efficiency," said Laura Boudreau, a professor at \uc2 Columbia Business School\uc2  who studies firm productivity and employment relationships between companies and workers.\par \par \uc2 Drives for efficiency can backfire if they hurt the quality of employees' day-to-day jobs, Ms. Boudreau said. "How far can employers push, and is there a point where that starts to demotivate employees?"\par \par \uc2 Executives say a company can become bloated over time if organizations aren't careful. Mr. Zuckerberg described how a low-priority project at \uc2 Meta\uc2  might siphon resources from throughout the company. Such a project would require a leader, potentially taking someone effective away from another team. It could require technical tools, space in an office, laptops and benefits from human resources.\par \par \uc2 Managers may decide they want more engineers to help on the project, leading the company to hire more IT, HR and recruiting people, which in turn leaves those teams bigger, less efficient and less responsive to higher-priority work, Mr. Zuckerberg wrote. "Indirect costs compound and it's easy to underestimate them."\par \par \uc2 Where employees work is also taking on added focus at \uc2 Meta\uc2 . The company embraced hybrid and remote work during the pandemic, giving many employees freedom to choose where they do their jobs. Though he said the company is committed to allowing both remote and hybrid work, Mr. Zuckerberg said \uc2 Meta\uc2  has observed that some employees could benefit from more time with their peers.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  also told employees in an internal message that it would pause all new remote-work applications and requests to transfer to another office through the first half of this year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg on Tuesday said engineers who joined the company in person and then began working remotely performed better, on average, than those who started remotely. He didn't share details on how the company judged performance. More junior engineers, earlier in their career, also perform better when working with teammates at least three days a week, Mr. Zuckerberg said, adding that it may be easier to build trust and develop relationships in person.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230316ej3g0000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
