\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 You Can't Fire Them, They Quit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Johnny Paycheck didn't have the guts to say "Take this job and shove it." You can't say the same thing for a lot of U.S. workers.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department on Wednesday reported that the number of unfilled U.S. jobs at the end of January slipped to a seasonally adjusted 10.82 million from December's 11.23 million, and the number of layoffs and discharges during January picked up to 1.72 million from 1.48 million a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 This suggests the job market might, in fact, be cooling -- though it is a bit like saying a 93-degree day is cooler than 98-degree one. There are still far more job openings than there were before the pandemic. There are fewer layoffs, too, and it looks as if many people who are getting laid off are finding other work quickly. The report showed that there were more people hired in January than in December.\par \par \uc2 Lately, there has been some concern about the accuracy of the Labor Department's job opening and turnover statistics because fewer employers are responding to its surveys, but the report was still consistent with other recent data.\par \par \uc2 An index of overall U.S. job openings from job-listing site Indeed has slipped but, as of early this month, was still a third higher than before the pandemic. And weekly initial jobless claims figures, which come from data collected by state agencies, are still extremely low, suggesting that many people who are laid off are finding new employment quickly.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's report also showed 3.88 million workers quit their jobs in January, down from December's 4.09 million. That is still high. Moreover, quits in the two industry categories with the lowest average hourly earnings -- leisure and hospitality and retail trade -- rose to 1.47 million from 1.4 million. That indicates that, among low-wage workers, there is still a lot of job churn.\par \par \uc2 It is a plus for those workers' earnings, and an integral part of why wage growth among lower-paid workers has been outstripping that of higher-income workers. A wage growth tracker developed by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2  shows that the 12-month moving average of annualized monthly wage growth for workers who switched jobs was 7.7% as of January, which compared with 5.5% for those who didn't change jobs.\par \par \uc2 This dynamic is helping to reverse decades of widening income inequality. Even though the better-paying new positions that quitters are taking might be at more efficient employers who can more easily afford them, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will worry that their wage gains could fuel inflation instead.\par \par \uc2 So it will keep raising rates until it is satisfied that the economy has really cooled. The danger is that in doing so it might eventually lead everybody to start singing the blues.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich392\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5880
