\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Low-Wage Workers Climb Salary Ladder --- Pandemic drives poorer, less-educated employees into better jobs, research suggests\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 759 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 When the Covid crisis struck, economists worried poorer workers might suffer a permanent setback. With the job losses the pandemic set off concentrated in low-wage industries, the earnings gap between the rich and the poor seemed likely to only widen.\par \par \uc2 It narrowed instead, with wage growth among lower-paid and less-educated workers outstripping wage growth among the better-paid and more highly educated. In a paper posted to the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2 's website on Monday, economists \uc2 David Autor\uc2  of the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 , and Annie McGrew and \uc2 Arindrajit Dube\uc2  of the \uc2 University of Massachusetts Amherst\uc2  explore how this came about. Their findings suggest even as the pandemic fades, competition for low-wage workers will be more intense than before the pandemic. That could lead to further reductions in income inequality, raise labor costs at companies that employ low-wage workers and reshape the business landscape.\par \par \uc2 The outsize wage gains that accrued to lower-income and less-educated workers since the pandemic began are well documented. Labor Department data show that for a worker at the upper limit of the bottom 10% by income, typical weekly earnings in the fourth quarter were 22.3% higher than in the fourth quarter of 2019, outpacing the 15.7% gain in consumer prices over the same period by the department's inflation measure. But for a worker at the 90th percentile, earnings grew 13.3%. The data show median weekly earnings for workers with just a high-school diploma rose 17.1% over the period, while for workers with at least a bachelor's degree they grew 11.9%.\par \par \uc2 But poorer workers aren't doing uniformly better. Analyzing microdata data from the household survey the Labor Department uses to help gauge the labor market, the economists find that among workers with a high-school education or less, it is the young -- those under 40 -- who have seen the most significant wage gains. That appears to be driven by young workers' higher tendency to switch jobs. Older, less-educated workers who stayed in the same job through the pandemic aren't keeping ahead of inflation like the young and footloose. "If you're stuck in the same place, your purchasing power is getting eroded," Mr. Autor says.\par \par \uc2 Part of what might have driven the wage gains is that the onset of the pandemic unstuck many less-educated workers from jobs that paid worse than they might have gotten elsewhere. The job losses among workers age 25 and older with a high-school education or less were extreme, with employment levels falling more than one-fifth from February 2020 to April 2020. But when the job market came roaring back, many of these workers didn't simply go back to their old jobs, but to ones with higher pay. Helping this process, relief payments from the government might have given them the wherewithal to shop for a better job. And seeing other people secure better-paying jobs might have led other workers to go on the hunt.\par \par \uc2 More formally, the underlying dynamic appears to go beyond a simple model where rising demand relative to the supply of workers raises wages. Rather, it is emblematic of what is called a job-ladder model, where in a tighter labor market, workers get more contacts from potential employers, providing the less-well-paid with more opportunities to switch to better paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Autor doesn't expect the fading of some of the pandemic's effects on the economy to unravel the gains made by less-educated workers. A dishwasher who landed a better-paying warehouse job but then goes back to being a dishwasher might be a better-paid dishwasher than before. Indeed, with the U.S. facing a demographic crunch that will limit the availability of younger workers in the years ahead, what has happened since early in the pandemic is "a premonition of how the labor market will be evolving in the next decade," he says.\par \par \uc2 For businesses that employ low-wage workers, this will present a challenge. Those that pay less than their competitors might need to pay more, and accept lower profit margins as a result. Those with razor-thin margins already will need to figure out how to become more efficient to afford workers and remain profitable. And those that are unable to become more efficient could simply go away.\par \par \uc2 page,5043 \par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230307ej3700019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
