\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Waller Says a Price-Rise Revival Can't Be Risked\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will need to raise rates to higher levels than previously anticipated to prevent inflation from picking up if the recent strength in hiring and consumer spending continues, a central bank official said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "I would be very pleased if the data we receive on inflation and the labor market this month show signs of moderation," Fed governor \uc2 Christopher Waller\uc2  said in remarks posted on the Fed's website. "But wishful thinking is not a substitute for hard evidence in the form of economic data. After seeing promising signs of progress, we cannot risk a revival of inflation."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Waller didn't say in his prepared remarks whether he would continue to favor raising interest rates by a quarter-percentage point, which was his preference at the Fed's last meeting, or whether he would instead support a larger half-point increase at its next gathering, March 21-22.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's rate-setting committee voted unanimously last month to slow rate increases by lifting their benchmark federal-funds rate by a quarter percentage point -- to a range between 4.5% and 4.75% -- following larger moves of a half point in December and 0.75 point in November.\par \par \uc2 At their December meeting, most Fed officials projected lifting the rate to between 5% and 5.5% this year to combat high inflation by slowing economic activity. So far, three officials out of 18 who participate in policy-setting deliberations have suggested they would have favored an increase of a half-percentage-point at the recent meeting or could support such a move this month.\par \par \uc2 Since Fed officials' recent meeting that concluded on Feb. 1, the government released reports showing stronger hiring and spending in January than forecast, along with slower progress on reducing inflation.\par \par \uc2 Recent data shows consumer spending isn't slowing as much as anticipated, that the job market "continues to run unsustainably hot, and that inflation is not coming down as fast as I had thought," said Mr. Waller on Thursday. "It could be that progress has stalled, or it is possible that the numbers released last month were a blip, perhaps associated with unusually favorable weather, and that forthcoming data will show that economic activity and inflation resumed their decline."\par \par \uc2 Separately, \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  told reporters Thursday that he "still very firmly" supports raising rates in quarter-point increments as officials determine just how high rates should rise to restrain economic activity. "Slow and steady in this is going to be the appropriate course of action," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230303ej330001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
