\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Biden to Nominate Deputy to Lead Labor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Restuccia and Ken Thomas \par \uc2 237 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Biden said he would nominate Julie Su, the No. 2 official at the Labor Department, to lead the agency, maintaining continuity within a department that played a prominent role in averting a rail strike last year.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Su, the current deputy secretary, was widely seen as the leading candidate to succeed departing Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 . Asian-American lawmakers and advocacy groups threw their support behind her, lobbying Mr. Biden to pick his first Asian-American cabinet secretary. Ms. Su, age 54, is Chinese-American.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden said Ms. Su has been "a champion for workers, and she has been a critical partner to Secretary Walsh since the early days of my administration." White House officials noted that Ms. Su played a central role in the Labor Department's negotiations with labor and freight rail companies late last year.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana Sen. \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , the top Republican on the Senate Health, Education, Labor and Pensions Committee, said Ms. Su was overseeing Labor Department rules that would hurt the so-called gig economy, and that she supervised California's unemployment insurance office when it paid out fraudulent unemployment benefits, as occurred in many other states. \uc2 Mary Kay Henry\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , said: "She understands the urgency of empowering workers to improve their lives in this rapidly changing economy."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230301ej310001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
