\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Inmates in Solitary Wage Hunger Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell \par \uc2 628 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For 17 years, Guadalupe Constante has been alone in a 5-foot-by-9-foot cell, spending hours at a time pacing the small space, he said.\par \par \uc2 The 44-year-old, who is midway through a 35-year sentence for aggravated robbery, is one of the Texas state prisoners held in solitary confinement because of membership in prison gangs. Last month, he and dozens of others stopped eating.\par \par \uc2 The hunger strike has moved among prison units, seeking to draw attention to solitary confinement practices that inmates and their advocates say are cruel and inhumane. "No contact other than with the guards putting on handcuffs," Mr. Constante wrote this month. "I think people are finally waking up to try to get change."\par \par \uc2 A month after more than 106 Texas state prisoners began refusing food, 12 were actively hunger striking Wednesday, meaning they had stopped eating for at least three days, according to the prison system. The strikes come as a new lawsuit is targeting Texas solitary confinement of prisoners on death row.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 600 inmates in Texas have spent more than a decade in solitary confinement -- making the statean extreme outlier among the 35 states in the analysis -- according to a study by Yale Law School last year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Texas Department of Criminal Justice\uc2  said it uses such housing judiciously. The department said that less than 3% of Texas prisoners are held in solitary confinement and that the population has dropped 65% since 2007.\par \par \uc2 As of 2021, Texas was one of 12 states that automatically placed men on death row in long-term solitary confinement.\par \par \uc2 Advocates of prison reform say extended solitary confinement is unacceptable. Lasting more than 15 consecutive days, it is internationally recognized by the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and human-rights organizations as torture. Additionally, they say, it causes extreme mental-health problems to inmates.\par \par \uc2 There were 3,141 Texas prisoners in solitary as of November; such inmates are kept in their cells 22 to 24 hours a day, with periodic removal for showers or solitary recreation. They are able to communicate with people outside via paid messages on tablets, or letters.\par \par \uc2 The department attributed the hunger strike to gang members attempting to gain power within the prison system. "If known prison gang members in state custody don't like their current confinement conditions, they are free to renounce their gang and we will offer them a pathway back into the general population," the department wrote in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Inmates said the program to renounce gang membership requires inmates to provide information on other members, putting themselves and their families in danger.\par \par \uc2 The hunger strikers are calling for the department to make solitary-confinement policies based on inmate behavior, rather than gang affiliation, said Brittany Robertson, a prison-reform advocate.\par \par \uc2 No one has maintained the strike longer than 20 days, according to the prison system. At least four men required medical intervention, a spokeswoman said last week. One of them was Edwin Schneider, 61, who is serving a life sentence for aggravated robbery and has spent over a decade in solitary confinement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schneider said in a message that he felt close to death after 12 days. On day 14, Mr. Schneider said he gave in, accepted a medical IV and began to eat again.\par \par \uc2 The hunger strike unfolded as prisoners on death row began their own challenge to Texas confinement practices. A federal lawsuit filed Jan. 26 argues that the Texas blanket policy of keeping men on death row in solitary confinement is unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, filed initially on behalf of four inmates, is designed to become a class-action suit on behalf of all 185 men currently sentenced to death in Texas, attorneys said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230211ej2b0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
