\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Hospitality and Healthcare Led to Employment Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon \par \uc2 512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employment surged in January, with widespread gains across most sectors as the job market remained a bastion of strength in an otherwise slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 Employers in leisure and hospitality led January's job growth, as restaurants, bars and hotels hired at a robust pace. Healthcare organizations snapped up workers last month to fill roles at doctors' offices and outpatient facilities. Professional-and-business services, which includes many white-collar jobs in accounting, consulting and architecture, also hired at a fast pace, Friday's Labor Department report showed.\par \par \uc2 The construction industry added jobs in January, though most of the sector's strength was concentrated in nonresidential construction.\par \par \uc2 The information sector shed jobs for the second straight month, further evidence of weakness in the tech sector. Payrolls in finance and insurance edged up by 1,100 in January from a month earlier. Banks and other financial institutions have reported layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at the January job gains and losses in several industries, according to the Labor Department's report:\par \par \uc2 Hospitality\par \par \uc2 Leisure-and-hospitality employers powered last month's job growth. The sector added 128,000 jobs in January, up from 2022's average of 89,000 a month. Restaurants and bars pumped out jobs. The continued strength in the sector reflects, in part, a catch-up with demand. Businesses in leisure and hospitality struggled to fill roles for months after consumers rushed to travel and eat out again in mid-2021. Payrolls in leisure and hospitality remain below prepandemic February 2020 levels by 495,000, or 2.9%.\par \par \uc2 Construction\par \par \uc2 The construction industry added 25,000 jobs in January, a touch above the 2022 monthly average of 22,000. Nonresidential contractor payrolls grew by 16,500, driving last month's gain in construction.\par \par \uc2 The sector wasn't bulking up due to government spending on infrastructure: Construction jobs for roadwork were down last month, said \uc2 Anirban Basu\uc2 , chief economist at Associated Builders and Contractors. Instead, hiring was strong last month for work at privately financed projects such as data centers, fulfillment centers and manufacturing facilities, Mr. Basu noted.\par \par \uc2 "I am astonished by the strength of the nonresidential construction labor market," Mr. Basu said. "With interest rates rising so profoundly last year, one would have expected the segment to have softened by now. Instead, many contractors report operating at capacity."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, residential contractors added 5,400 jobs in January, a 0.2% increase from December and below the 0.6% rise for nonresidential contracting. Over the past year, residential contracting jobs have grown strongly.\par \par \uc2 Tech and finance\par \par \uc2 There are signs of weakness in sectors such as tech and finance, which have reported layoffs in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The information sector was one of the few sectors to cut jobs last month. Employment among telecommunications companies and firms that provide data-processing services declined in January from a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Pockets of weakness also emerged in the finance sector, where payrolls fell month-over-month in commercial banking, credit-related services and insurance.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230204ej2400020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
