\par\par \b \uc2 How a Real-Estate Entrepreneur Saved Britain From Itself\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Yergin \par \uc2 927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Once again, Britain is caught in a paralyzing "winter of discontent" as rail workers, nurses, airport immigration officers, school teachers, mail carriers, bus drivers, university professors, highway officers and ambulance drivers go on strike. Britain has not seen so much labor unrest since the 1970s, when garbage collectors stayed home and grave diggers refused to bury the dead. Conditions were so dire in those years that the country had to be bailed out by a loan from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , and there was talk that Britain would end up the "East Germany of the Western world."\par \par \uc2 That paralyzing turmoil set the stage for the Thatcher Revolution, which over several years reduced government domination of Britain's economy and got it working again. One of the leaders of that Revolution was David Young, who recently passed away at age 90. Young served Thatcher as an adviser, campaign manager and cabinet minister. He was an architect of the privatization that transformed the country's economy, and his political career offers lessons for today's turmoil.\par \par \uc2 In 1995 -- five years after Thatcher left office -- I ran into Young and his wife, Lita, on a vacation. That accidental meeting led me to think about the book that became "The Commanding Heights" and then a PBS and BBC television series. The theme became the battle and shifting balance between governments and markets that was central to the Thatcher Revolution -- and that, once again, is being played out around the world.\par \par \uc2 This is the story the Youngs told me. In the mid-1970s he was a real estate entrepreneur. He had become deeply unhappy with the economic disarray in Britain and pained by the hostility exhibited by many to the very idea of a market economy. So great was the animosity to markets that he took care to avoid letting people know that he was an entrepreneur. "It was not socially acceptable to work for oneself," he said.\par \par \uc2 He concluded that the only way out was to leave -- to emigrate to the United States and start over. What ensued would likely prove to be the shortest emigration on record.\par \par \uc2 Young and his wife flew to Boston and checked in to the \uc2 Ritz-Carlton\uc2 , which looks out on Boston's Public Garden. They were startled to wake up on their first day, Sunday morning, to the sound of sirens. Later that day they set off for what they assumed would be their first walk in their new country, a pleasant ramble on Boston Common. Instead, they found themselves crying -- the effect of tear gas, loosed by police to break up a riot over the bitterly divisive court-enforced busing of school children, which was tearing the city apart.\par \par \uc2 That was it. "You must be out of your mind," Lita said to him as they rubbed their eyes with handkerchiefs. "You're crazy if you think I'm going to give up my family for this."\par \par \uc2 They went back to the hotel and got on the phone to an airline. They left that night for London. On the flight, Young ruminated that there ought to be something more he could do beyond despair and depart. That meant politics. He had been a Labour voter, but the Labour Party was presiding over an economic disaster. \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  had just been elected Leader of the Conservative Party, and she had new and different ideas about the dismal British economy and the need to reopen it. Those ideas appealed to him.\par \par \uc2 Back in London, he connected with a Conservative politician Keith Joseph, whom Thatcher was to describe as "my closet political friend." Young became part of the inner circle around Thatcher. As prime minister she appointed him to a series of ministerial and other senior positions. His remit: to restart the economy, reduce unemployment -- and, by the by, make entrepreneurship socially acceptable again.\par \par \uc2 Young was the driving force behind the government's efforts to privatize large segments of the British economy by turning loss-making state-owned companies into enterprises owned by shareholders.\par \par \uc2 It was an arduous job -- politically, financially and organizationally. The phone company, for example, had to be extracted out of the post office. Altogether, said Young, "the Thatcher years turned the United Kingdom from being a producer-led economy into a consumer-led economy, and it was becoming a competitive economy." No more East Germany.\par \par \uc2 Young also championed the "Chunnel," the undersea tunnel that connects England and France by train, and he was the one who pushed the button on the first drill to start the digging of that massive project.\par \par \uc2 "Other people come to me with their problems," Thatcher said in 1984. "David Young comes with his achievements." In her memoir she remarked that he "understood how to make things happen." But none of those things would have happened without Young's quick turn-around from his would-be emigration to America.\par \par \uc2 There is a lesson here. With Britain once again engulfed in labor strife and with governments around the world expanding their roles in the economy, current British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  needs people like David Young who don't just identify problems but can create solutions in Britain's' new era of economic disarray and budgetary shortfalls -- solutions that are focused on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yergin, vice chairman of S&P Global, is author of "The New Map: Energy, Climate and the Clash of Nations" and a co-author of "The Commanding Heights: The Battle for the World Economy."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230204ej2400014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
