\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Amazon Workers In U.K. Walk Out\b0\par\par\uc2 By Trefor Moss \par \uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . workers in Britain went on strike for the first time Wednesday, dragging the e-commerce giant into the wave of labor unrest that has been sweeping the U.K. and other European economies.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said the industrial action at a warehouse in Coventry in central England would have little impact on its overall operations, the strikes could portend further disruption for the company in one of its most important overseas markets. Union leaders have already vowed to extend strikes to other \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities in the U.K. in a dispute over pay.\par \par \uc2 Around 300 workers out of roughly 2,000 at the Coventry site were taking part in the walkout Wednesday, according to the \uc2 GMB Union\uc2 , which organized the industrial action.The union added that it is planning further strikes at the facility, and that other \uc2 Amazon\uc2  sites in the U.K. would soon be balloted over potential industrial action. The company says it employs over 70,000 people in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 Britain has been experiencing its biggest strikes in decades this winter, with doctors and nurses, postal workers, railway staff and teachers among those demanding improved pay deals amid a painful surge in living costs.\par \par \uc2 Workers in France, Germany and Italy also have taken industrial action in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The strike at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in the U.K. comes after the retailer's workers in Staten Island, New York, voted last year to form the company's first American union. However, the threat of widespread worker unrest then dissipated as employees at several other U.S. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities voted against unionization.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the Coventry facility in England voted to go on strike last year after some deemed an hourly pay increase of 50 pence, the equivalent of 62 cents, to be inadequate, according to the \uc2 GMB Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it pays its British workers at least GBP 10.50 an hour. That is more than the nationally mandated minimum wage of GBP 9.50 an hour, though that is set to rise to GBP 10.45 an hour in April.\par \par \uc2 "People working for one of the most valuable companies in the world shouldn't have to threaten strike action just to win a wage they can live on," the \uc2 GMB Union\uc2  said. The union, which represents half a million workers across a range of industries, said it wants \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers to be paid an hourly rate of GBP 15.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said its U.K. workers receive "competitive pay," and that basic pay for its staff in the country had risen 29% since 2018.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230126ej1q00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
