\par\par \b \uc2 Walmart\uc2  to Raise Starting Hourly Wages to $14\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer \par \uc2 416 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 A Business & Finance article on Wednesday about Walmart Inc. incorrectly said that the federal minimum wage is set to increase to $9.50 an hour on July 1 from $7.25; no change is scheduled. Also, Target Corp. raised starting wages for its hourly workers to $15 in 2020. The article incorrectly said Target raised those wages last year.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ January 26, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 Walmart Inc\uc2 . said it is raising wages for its U.S. hourly workers as the country's largest private employer continues to fight to attract staff in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 Starting next month, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's U.S. workers in stores and warehouses will earn a starting wage of at least $14 an hour, up from $12, the company said in a memo to staff on Tuesday. Rivals, including \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Target Corp\uc2 ., have a $15-an-hour minimum wage. The federal minimum wage is set to increase to $9.50 an hour on July 1, from $7.25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's changes, the latest in a series of increases to close the gap with rivals, will push the company's average hourly wages to over $17.50. Currently hourly workers earn an average of around $17, a spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. job market has been tight, with an unemployment rate of 3.5% in December matching multidecade lows. There are signs that the labor market is losing momentum as hiring and wage growth cooled in December, though the number of job openings still far outpaces the number of unemployed people.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  continues to confront a tight labor market for some of its core job types, including store workers and truck drivers. Around 340,000 of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's 1.6 million U.S. workers will see a raise in their March paychecks related to the change, the spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which recently announced plans to cut more than 18,000 jobs, mostly in its corporate ranks, said in September that it was increasing average starting pay for its warehouse employees to more than $19 from $18 an hour. Target raised starting wages for its hourly workers to $15 last year.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has a more rural and southeastern store base compared with some of its U.S. rivals. That means more of its 4,600 stores and warehouses are in states such as Texas, Mississippi and Arkansas that haven't adopted higher minimum wages in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230125ej1p00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
