\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 Remote Work Puts Dent in Washington's Office-Space Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Grant \par \uc2 681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The federal government's easygoing return-to-office policy is enabling the majority of employees of Washington, D.C.'s biggest employer to work remotely most of the time, hurting the city's office market, real-estate executives say.\par \par \uc2 Many big-city mayors have been requiring city workers back in the office for the bulk of the workweek and urging companies to do the same. But return-to-work directives for the country's more than four million federal workers have been relatively muted, in part because the government says it sees allowing for remote work as a competitive advantage for retaining workers.\par \par \uc2 About 5% of the prepandemic workforce returned to work in federally leased buildings in Washington in October and November, according to an analysis by commercial real-estate services firm \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 . The average return-to-office rate for all office space users in the 10 major metro areas monitored by \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2  is closer to 50%. Kastle records swipes by building access cards.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, the office vacancy rate is at a record 19.5%, according to \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The federal government's overall unwillingness to return en masse is having all sorts of downstream effects in the broader real-estate community," said Nathan Edwards, senior research director at \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Biden in his State of the Union address last year said, "It's time for America to get back to work and fill our great downtowns again with people."\par \par \uc2 But in practice, many federal agencies are encouraging telework and hybrid workplace plans that allow employees to work most or all of their time from home. Agencies are adopting these strategies to cut costs and enhance efficiency, said a spokeswoman for the Office of Management and Budget. In a hot market for workers, flexible work plans "allow the federal government to better compete in the labor market," the spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 The list of cities that have called city workers back to downtown offices more than half the time includes New York, Washington, Chicago, Houston, San Francisco and Boston, according to officials in those cities.\par \par \uc2 The spokeswoman at the Office of Management and Budget declined to provide a statistic on how office usage currently compares with prepandemic levels. Instead, she and a spokesman for the federal Office of Personnel Management each cited a report by the personnel agency that stated 47% of all federal employees nationwide participated in telework in fiscal 2021.\par \par \uc2 The less-urgent federal response has been frustrating for District of Columbia Mayor Muriel Bowser, who held a "welcome back" celebration in 2021 for city government workers. Bowser administration officials have noted that the federal government accounts for one in four jobs in the city and have called for a clear directive from President Biden for a widespread return.\par \par \uc2 While the Washington region has the largest number of federal employees, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  also maintains facilities in about 2,200 other communities, noted a letter this past month to Mr. Biden from the Real Estate Roundtable, a trade group that has pushed for a more vigorous return-to-office policy. Actions the federal government takes "have profound impacts on local markets," the letter states.\par \par \uc2 Since 2010, Congress, the Office of Management and Budget and other federal agencies have been implementing initiatives to reduce the federal government's space usage to save money, according to a September report by the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2 . In 2015, the federal government leased about 193 million square feet of office space from private-sector landlords, according to Victor Calanog, head of commercial real-estate economics of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics. This past year, that number was 180 million square feet, Mr. Calanog said.\par \par \uc2 In the Washington region, federal agencies typically are taking 25% to 30% less space when they renew their leases, according to \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 . This past year, the \uc2 U.S. Patent and Trademark Office\uc2  said it would shed about one-third of its space in northern Virginia, or about 800,000 square feet.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich425\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6375
