\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Union Backs Pact And Ends Strike At CNH Plants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 376 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union workers at two plants for \uc2 CNH Industrial NV\uc2  ratified a new contract with the farm and construction equipment maker, ending a strike that began in early May.\par \par \uc2 About 1,100 members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  at plants in Racine, Wis., and Burlington, Iowa, will start returning to work Jan. 30, the company said. The workers walked off their jobs May 2, after rejecting an offer from the company that union leaders said provided insufficient wage increases and higher employee expenses for health insurance coverage.\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2  shares closed up 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 London-based \uc2 CNH\uc2 , whose brands include \uc2 Case IH\uc2  and New Holland, is the second-largest farm equipment manufacturer in the U.S. behind \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . The company said the new contract would provide wage increases of 25% to 38% over the life of the four-year deal.\par \par \uc2 The union said in a statement the new contract provides wage increases, work-shift premium pay increases and job classification upgrades. The union described the ratified contract as an improvement over a proposal the workers voted down earlier this month, which the company had described as its "last, best and final offer" and included the wage increases of 25% to 38% over four years.\par \par \uc2 The company had urged the union to reconsider the offer. Union negotiators asked the company to continue bargaining sessions. \uc2 CNH\uc2  said U.S. Labor Secretary Martin Walsh conducted a mediation session with company and union negotiators before the latest vote on the offer.\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Scott Wine\uc2  credited Mr. Walsh with helping "us navigate the complexities of the negotiation process and end the strike."\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2  workers had complained throughout the strike that nonunion production workers at \uc2 CNH\uc2  were being paid more than union-represented workers had been. The \uc2 CNH\uc2  workers' push for bigger pay increases drew momentum after members of the \uc2 UAW union\uc2  received a new contract from \uc2 Deere\uc2  last fall, after workers at that company struck for the first time in 35 years.\par \par \uc2 CNH\uc2 , like other equipment manufacturers, has struggled to obtain key components in short supply during the past 18 months. Some dealers said the strike further lengthened wait times for new machinery and components.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230124ej1o0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
