\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Some Groups' Wages Far Outpace Prices --- Median earnings rose by 7.4% in 2022, with biggest gains for Black and younger workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gabriel T. Rubin \par \uc2 656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Black workers, young workers and people on the bottom of the income scale were among those who saw the largest pay increases last year, when employers were readily handing out raises in a tight labor market and high inflation environment.\par \par \uc2 Median weekly earnings for all workers were 7.4% higher, year over year, at the end of 2022, according to an analysis of newly released Labor Department data. That outpaced the consumer inflation rate of 7.1% in the fourth quarter, from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 For some workers, the gains were larger. The median raise for Black Americans employed full time was 11.3%, compared with the prior year. Weekly pay for workers aged 16 to 24 rose more than 10%. The bottom 10th of wage earners -- those that make about $570 a week -- saw their pay increase by nearly 10%.\par \par \uc2 The pay increases were welcome relief for those groups who have experienced "decades of pain" in the labor market, said Aaron Sojourner, an economist at the Upjohn Institute for Employment Research.\par \par \uc2 "It's been a struggle for those folks to find a productive place in the community where they can contribute and earn and build families and success."\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at which Americans saw the largest, and smallest, raises in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Wages by education\par \par \uc2 Better pay raises late last year went to workers who attended college, a reversal from earlier in the pandemic when those who hadn't completed high school saw outsize gains.\par \par \uc2 The annual rate of wage growth for workers with less than a high school diploma touched a recent peak in the second quarter of 2022, when it was up 11.1% over the prior year, higher than the 7.6% wage growth during that period for workers with a bachelor's degree or higher.\par \par \uc2 But by the end of 2022, the median worker without a high-school diploma was making $675 a week, or 3.7% more than during the last quarter of 2021. The median worker with at least a bachelor's degree saw 5.5% wage growth from a year earlier to $1,547 a week.\par \par \uc2 Wages by race\par \par \uc2 The median Black worker's weekly pay increased at the fastest rate among those measured, growing to $896 from $805 a year earlier. The median Hispanic or Latino worker saw a 4.8% raise to $837 a week. White and Asian workers saw wage growth slightly higher than the overall workforce.\par \par \uc2 Still, white and Asian workers continued to earn far more than other racial groups. Median weekly earnings for full-time Asian workers was $1,496, and white workers earned $1,111.\par \par \uc2 Wages by age group\par \par \uc2 The rate of raises between workers ages 16 to 24 and other age groups widened in 2022.\par \par \uc2 It isn't unusual for young workers to experience faster wage growth in percentage terms, largely because they typically start from the lowest base.\par \par \uc2 That youngest cohort, earning a median of $716 a week, ended the year with 10.8% annual wage growth, compared with 7.2% for the next-strongest group, aged 25 to 54.\par \par \uc2 Older workers, those in the 55-64 bracket and those over 65, who often start from higher bases, saw the slowest wage growth last year, at 2.8% and 3.9%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Wages by earnings level\par \par \uc2 The lowest-paid workers saw larger raises last year than the top 10% of wage earners.\par \par \uc2 Those in the bottom 10% saw weekly pay grow 9.8% from a year earlier at the end of 2022, to $571 a week. The top 10% received a raise of 5.7%, to $2,584 a week.\par \par \uc2 Gains for low-income and low-skilled workers drove broader wage growth throughout the post-reopening period of the pandemic.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich494\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7410
