\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Unemployment Lasts Longer as Hiring Cools\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon \par \uc2 560 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Jeffrey Durante was laid off from a financial technology company in August, he hoped he could land a new job by November.\par \par \uc2 But come mid-January, Mr. Durante was still searching for a product-manager job or related role. And he isn't alone.\par \par \uc2 Unemployed Americans across the U.S. are spending more time out of work as employers slow down hiring from a red-hot pace earlier in the pandemic. In December, 826,000 unemployed workers had been out of a job for about 3 1/2 to 6 months, up from 526,000 in April 2022, according to the Labor Department. Earlier this month, the number of people seeking ongoing unemployment benefits, known as continuing claims, was 26% above half-century lows reached last spring.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Durante has broadened his search from tech companies to include firms across several industries. The 28-year-old, who resides in the Washington, D.C., area, said he interviewed with five companies that told him they were putting the positions on hold amid budget cuts and hiring freezes.\par \par \uc2 "It just is a gut punch honestly after getting told that you're good enough for the job but they can't hire you," Mr. Durante said.\par \par \uc2 Companies started dialing back on hiring last year, in part reflecting heightened uncertainty in the face of Federal Reserve interest-rate hikes. The pullback in hiring helps explain why some job seekers are remaining unemployed for longer despite a strong overall labor market with historically high job openings.\par \par \uc2 "If you're somebody who has lost a job, your opportunities of finding one quickly have diminished somewhat since the spring," said Guy Berger, principal economist at networking site \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When employers turn cautious about the economic outlook, they are more likely to first pull back on hiring than they are to resort to layoffs, Mr. Berger said. Such a dynamic occurred during previous slowdowns, he said.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear whether the pool of unemployed workers will expand in the coming months. The unemployment rate was at a half-century low of 3.5% in December. Firms in lower-wage sectors such as restaurants, bars and hotels might be reluctant to cut workers they struggled to hire during the pandemic rebound.\par \par \uc2 But demand for labor is weakening fastest in white-collar industries. Layoffs in finance, real estate and tech are rising from historically low levels as companies that bulked up earlier in the pandemic shed employees.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Herhilan, 44, has submitted close to 2,000 applications since her contractor job in human resources ended over the summer. She is casting a wide net, applying for human-resources jobs and others including a customer-service position at a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  store and an assembly role at a small manufacturing plant.\par \par \uc2 The Port Sanilac, Mich., resident is finding that the competition is much stiffer than in the latter half of 2021, when she was also unemployed. For a remote position in human resources, she has recently seen 800 other applicants for the same job on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Herhilan exhausted Michigan's allotted 20 weeks of unemployment benefits and is running through savings.\par \par \uc2 "I'm very, very frustrated and extremely stressed and anxious because I don't know when I'm going to land a job," she said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230123ej1n00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
